#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cbb7321-753d-5253-8b64-072e3cc227bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6101" :question/problem "Represent the type I region above using set notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/46d316127c71bdc5d921d5efb296b113.png)" :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x$.\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1$.\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x$.\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/196068d46324da2557e27acfe2e6f5d5.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function\n\n$$\ny=x+1\n$$\n\nand leaves through the upper function $y=7-x$. So, we have\n\n$$\nx+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "cce0a7b2-7b25-5da6-8aa2-cbe8a40cd522" :question/math-academy-id "e-6102" :question/problem "Represent the type II region above using set notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/527c08bc412d4f54db57900a3bb5d731.png)" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: a \\leq y \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y$.\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}$. Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y$.\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/a1e788a35779364579390f7c39e5532d.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2-y}\n$$\n\nand leaves through the upper function\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/id #uuid "cd9ce5bf-1663-5110-8aaa-8f0f47d7d30a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6103" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = \\pm\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/dc17a2cba3c4044e9c97228407b26379.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}$.\nSo, this is a type I region.\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}$.\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c31a21fa-78d0-5402-ad6e-7909de28c734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128439} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cef178-b4aa-5972-a3c5-d9a90f0d1ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128440} {:answer/id #uuid "067b4703-a66d-5387-a004-5cc61294cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128441} {:answer/id #uuid "264f720c-645d-505a-9ef6-6cf33de45b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128442} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8df843-7067-5ec7-a6aa-166bd4f324d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067b4703-a66d-5387-a004-5cc61294cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71809-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128441} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ddbb2d-f20e-57c1-ac88-8cd8ef8a8357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128438}] :question/id #uuid "fc996932-2b25-55a2-8867-4e8b12518ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71809" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type I region in two-dimensional space?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8045847e-bce2-51fa-9b74-1f53019691b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128445} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d01c6d-7500-585c-a277-6ad16e49cbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{2} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128446} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3e89be-2db1-5fee-8ed1-3b1c5001ca45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{2} - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128447} {:answer/id #uuid "44c3ce81-dfe0-5d19-977d-e2c9cf10d3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{y - 3}, 1 \\le y \\le 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128448} {:answer/id #uuid "59614551-cf5d-5fbc-919e-4bc360630db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{y - 3}, 1 \\le y \\le 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d01c6d-7500-585c-a277-6ad16e49cbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le x^{2} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128446} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4d9b46-62d0-56cf-8dc7-0c5c38b6ca92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128444}] :question/id #uuid "4f6ac07a-f53a-5686-9107-a7ecc32ad390" :question/math-academy-id "q-71816" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71816.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7659b40-038f-5212-a604-37864d8f2982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128469} {:answer/id #uuid "f47e5dfd-337d-5984-a6dc-8389b0c341f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128470} {:answer/id #uuid "02f30174-62cd-5231-a37f-50e1641d3ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128471} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1616b2-4638-58ca-8044-4da91a99dc7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128472} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff40cdd-c66b-5b4a-aa58-d68b10bb0e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7659b40-038f-5212-a604-37864d8f2982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128469} :answer-field/id #uuid "131fca85-7a9e-54c7-96df-567631771b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128468}] :question/id #uuid "7805c1fc-f8dc-58be-9c44-d22a8226c755" :question/math-academy-id "q-71895" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles $x^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} = 1$ and $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region"}]}
